1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,610
>> At 3,000 rpm, a car engine's crankshaft turns 50 times a second.

2
00:00:06,690 --> 00:00:13,010
>> And yet each cylinder only burns fuel 25 times a second, which is once every two turns.

3
00:00:13,090 --> 00:00:19,530
Its piston makes four trips for every burn, and only one of those trips actually pushes.

4
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:22,470
>> That's four trips for one push. It sounds wasteful.

5
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:26,840
>> It does. And it's still the design inside most car engines.

6
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:31,520
>> It's the four-stroke cycle. It was first patented by a man who never built one

7
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:37,720
>> and made to work by a former grocery salesman, who later lost his patent.

8
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:44,490
>> Let's open one up. This is a simplified diagram of a single cylinder.

9
00:00:44,570 --> 00:00:48,580
>> So inside the cylinder there's a piston, sliding up and down.

10
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:52,790
>> And a connecting rod links that piston to the crankshaft, which turns.

11
00:00:52,870 --> 00:00:55,480
>> So up-and-down becomes round-and-round.

12
00:00:55,560 --> 00:01:02,290
>> Exactly. Up top there are two valves. One lets air and fuel in, and the other lets burnt gas out.

13
00:01:02,370 --> 00:01:06,980
>> And cams on a camshaft push them open, then springs close them again.

14
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:09,170
>> Between them sits the spark plug.

15
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:15,440
>> The piston's highest point is called top dead centre, and its lowest is called bottom dead centre.

16
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:19,840
>> At each of them the piston is momentarily at rest, before it turns around.

17
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:23,510
>> And one trip between them, up or down, is a stroke.

18
00:01:23,590 --> 00:01:29,320
>> Now watch the piston as the crank turns. It follows along, but not in a perfectly even rhythm.

19
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:31,710
>> It looks like a smooth wave to me.

20
00:01:31,790 --> 00:01:39,020
>> Nearly. But after a quarter turn, the piston has already covered more than half its stroke.

21
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:41,920
>> How can a quarter turn give you more than half?

22
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:49,010
>> The rod tilts. That makes the piston hurry through the top half of the stroke, and dawdle through the bottom.

23
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:56,530
>> Okay. Let's run one whole cycle, slowly, from the top.

24
00:01:56,610 --> 00:02:02,620
>> Stroke 1 is intake. The intake valve opens, and the falling piston draws in air and fuel.

25
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:06,210
>> It's pulled in by the partial vacuum it leaves behind.

26
00:02:06,290 --> 00:02:13,930
Stroke 2 is compression. Both valves shut, and the rising piston squeezes the mixture into about a tenth of the space.

27
00:02:14,010 --> 00:02:17,340
>> And by now the crankshaft has turned once, all the way round.

28
00:02:17,420 --> 00:02:21,860
>> Near the top, the spark plug fires, and the mixture starts to burn.

29
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:26,590
>> Stroke 3 is power. The hot, high-pressure gas drives the piston down.

30
00:02:26,670 --> 00:02:29,830
>> And that's the only stroke that pushes on the crank.

31
00:02:29,910 --> 00:02:33,070
>> The other three just get the gas in, ready, and out again.

32
00:02:33,150 --> 00:02:40,520
>> Stroke 4 is exhaust. The exhaust valve opens, and the rising piston pushes the burnt gas out.

33
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:45,950
>> Then it all starts again. That's four strokes, two turns of the crank, and one push.

34
00:02:46,030 --> 00:02:52,220
>> That's why the camshaft turns at half the crankshaft's speed, so that each valve opens once per cycle.

35
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:55,170
>> So it's two turns of the crank for one turn of the cam.

36
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:59,090
>> But what carries the piston through the three strokes that don't push?

37
00:02:59,170 --> 00:03:06,840
>> It's a heavy flywheel. It soaks up energy from each power stroke, and gives some back to squeeze the next charge.

38
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:15,110
So who came up with all this? The idea is older than the engine that made it famous.

39
00:03:15,190 --> 00:03:22,760
>> In 1860, Étienne Lenoir in Paris built a gas engine that burned its fuel without compressing it first.

40
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:26,070
>> And it turned only about 4% of the fuel's energy into work.

41
00:03:26,150 --> 00:03:29,470
>> Four percent. So almost all of it went to waste.

42
00:03:29,550 --> 00:03:36,490
>> Almost all. Then a travelling salesman in Germany, Nicolaus Otto, learned of it, and started building engines himself.

43
00:03:36,570 --> 00:03:44,260
>> In 1862 he tried one that compressed its charge first. It ran for a few minutes, then destroyed itself.

44
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:52,490
>> That same year, in France, Alphonse Beau de Rochas patented the four-stroke cycle. But he never built an engine.

45
00:03:52,570 --> 00:03:56,860
>> So the idea existed on paper before anyone made it run.

46
00:03:56,940 --> 00:04:03,010
>> Meanwhile, in 1864, Otto and Eugen Langen founded the first company devoted to making engines.

47
00:04:03,090 --> 00:04:10,980
>> Their atmospheric engine won a gold medal at the 1867 Paris World's Fair, at about 12% efficiency.

48
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:12,930
>> That's three times Lenoir's.

49
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:19,780
>> Then, in 1876, Otto's company built an engine that compressed its charge in four strokes.

50
00:04:19,860 --> 00:04:25,910
>> It was an immediate success, and the first commercially successful engine to compress its charge in the cylinder.

51
00:04:25,990 --> 00:04:33,760
>> And two of Otto's former engineers, Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach, made it small and fast enough for vehicles.

52
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:40,130
>> But in 1886 the patent was cancelled in Germany, because Beau de Rochas had patented the cycle first.

53
00:04:40,210 --> 00:04:44,650
>> So a patent for an engine nobody had built ended Otto's monopoly.

54
00:04:44,730 --> 00:04:48,240
>> And neither Otto nor Daimler had even known that patent existed.

55
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:55,280
>> Still, the engines spread. By 1889, more than 50 companies were building Otto-type engines.

56
00:04:55,360 --> 00:05:00,820
>> And the cycle still carries Otto's name. It's called the Otto cycle.

57
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:10,660
>> So why does squeezing first help so much? Engineers answer with a graph of pressure against volume.

58
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:16,000
>> This is the idealised version, the Otto cycle, traced as our engine turns.

59
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:20,270
>> During intake, the volume grows, at about the pressure outside.

60
00:05:20,350 --> 00:05:24,200
>> During compression, the volume shrinks tenfold, and the pressure climbs steeply.

61
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:30,850
>> Combustion is treated as instant, right at the top, so the pressure leaps while the volume stays the same.

62
00:05:30,930 --> 00:05:35,010
>> In a real engine the burn takes a little time, but this shape is the point.

63
00:05:35,090 --> 00:05:39,900
During the power stroke, the hot gas expands and pushes, and its pressure falls.

64
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:44,390
>> At the bottom, the exhaust valve opens, and the pressure drops back.

65
00:05:44,470 --> 00:05:49,380
>> During exhaust, the piston sweeps the gas out, and the loop closes.

66
00:05:49,460 --> 00:05:51,700
>> And what does the loop tell us?

67
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:59,320
>> The area inside it is the net work, which is what the power stroke gave, minus what the compression cost.

68
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:05,900
>> So squeezing costs some work, but the hot gas pays back much more on the way down.

69
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:14,000
>> That brings us to the compression ratio, which is the cylinder's largest volume divided by its smallest.

70
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:18,770
>> And in the ideal cycle, the efficiency depends on that ratio alone.

71
00:06:18,850 --> 00:06:26,000
>> Efficiency is 1 minus the ratio to the power 1 minus γ, where γ, gamma, is 1.4 for air.

72
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:27,900
>> Let's put some numbers in.

73
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:33,550
Squeeze four to one, and the ideal engine turns 43% of its heat into work.

74
00:06:33,630 --> 00:06:38,020
>> At ten to one, it's 60%, and at sixteen to one, it's 67%.

75
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:41,670
>> Sixty percent? Car engines don't get anywhere near that.

76
00:06:41,750 --> 00:06:49,290
>> No. Real engines lose energy to heat and friction along the way. A petrol engine turns about 30% into useful work.

77
00:06:49,370 --> 00:06:54,580
>> But the lesson holds. If you squeeze harder, you get more work from the same fuel.

78
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:58,310
>> So why do most petrol car engines stop at around 8 to 12 to one?

79
00:06:58,390 --> 00:07:03,330
>> Because squeezing a gas heats it, and a hot enough mixture can ignite by itself.

80
00:07:03,410 --> 00:07:07,910
>> Normally, the spark starts a flame front that sweeps across the chamber.

81
00:07:07,990 --> 00:07:13,880
>> But if the unburnt mixture ahead of the flame gets too hot, pockets of it explode all at once.

82
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:19,670
>> That's called knock. It causes a metallic pinging, and pressure spikes that can damage the engine.

83
00:07:19,750 --> 00:07:23,040
>> So it's knock, not the formula, that sets the limit.

84
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:30,240
>> Right. A fuel's octane rating measures how well it resists igniting by itself. Higher octane allows more squeeze.

85
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:37,420
>> And modern engines listen for knock with sensors, and fire the spark later when they hear it.

86
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:47,390
So far, everything has happened exactly at the top or bottom of a stroke. Real engines don't wait.

87
00:07:47,470 --> 00:07:52,410
>> The spark fires early, at some 10 to 40 degrees of crank rotation before the top.

88
00:07:52,490 --> 00:07:55,430
>> Before the piston has even finished squeezing?

89
00:07:55,510 --> 00:08:00,550
>> Yes. The flame takes time to burn, and the peak pressure should come just after the top.

90
00:08:00,630 --> 00:08:07,090
>> And the flame doesn't speed up with the engine, so at higher revs the spark needs a longer head start.

91
00:08:07,170 --> 00:08:11,480
>> The valves are early, too. They open before the end of a stroke, and close after it.

92
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:16,400
>> So at the end of the exhaust stroke, both valves are briefly open at once.

93
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:19,970
>> That's valve overlap, and it's designed in on purpose.

94
00:08:20,050 --> 00:08:22,040
>> Why would you want both open?

95
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:26,790
>> The outgoing exhaust is moving fast, and it helps draw the fresh charge in behind it.

96
00:08:26,870 --> 00:08:32,230
>> So the trick is to close the exhaust valve just as the fresh charge reaches it.

97
00:08:32,310 --> 00:08:40,190
>> Exactly. Racing engines use lots of overlap. Road engines, which must idle smoothly below 1,000 rpm, use less.

98
00:08:40,270 --> 00:08:47,690
>> And many now shift their camshafts as they run, to get both power at high revs and a smooth idle.

99
00:08:51,290 --> 00:08:56,080
>> One cylinder gives one push every two turns. That's a lumpy way to drive a car.

100
00:08:56,160 --> 00:09:01,450
>> So most car engines have several cylinders on one crankshaft, firing in turn.

101
00:09:01,530 --> 00:09:07,640
>> Spaced evenly, the power strokes come every 720 degrees divided by the number of cylinders.

102
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:13,860
>> With four cylinders, that's every 180 degrees, which gives a push every half turn.

103
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:19,390
>> And a straight four usually fires its cylinders in the order 1-3-4-2.

104
00:09:19,470 --> 00:09:21,280
>> Not one-two-three-four?

105
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:29,380
>> 1-3-4-2 is the usual order. Some British engines used 1-2-4-3.

106
00:09:29,460 --> 00:09:34,500
>> Here's the twisting force that the gas puts on the crank, in an idealised model.

107
00:09:34,580 --> 00:09:39,560
>> One cylinder gives one big shove, then a long wait, with a drag while it compresses.

108
00:09:39,640 --> 00:09:42,750
>> Four cylinders give one shove after another.

109
00:09:42,830 --> 00:09:48,330
>> With six cylinders, the power strokes overlap, and the push is smoother still.

110
00:09:48,410 --> 00:09:53,730
>> And even spacing means less vibration, and a smoother sound.

111
00:09:57,330 --> 00:10:01,070
There's another way to light the fire, with no spark at all.

112
00:10:01,150 --> 00:10:07,650
>> A diesel draws in only air, and squeezes it much harder, typically 15 to 23 to one.

113
00:10:07,730 --> 00:10:11,420
>> And squeezing air that hard heats it by hundreds of degrees.

114
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:16,450
>> Near the top, fuel is sprayed in, and it ignites in the hot air by itself.

115
00:10:16,530 --> 00:10:19,640
>> So the squeeze itself does the spark plug's job.

116
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:27,700
>> In a way. With no fuel in the cylinder during the squeeze, nothing can ignite early, so it can squeeze harder.

117
00:10:27,780 --> 00:10:34,370
>> That's one reason diesels are efficient. Big, slow ship diesels reach up to 55%.

118
00:10:34,450 --> 00:10:40,020
>> And instead of throttling its air, a diesel sets its power by how much fuel it injects.

119
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:50,910
>> Rudolf Diesel's first prototype fired in 1893, and his third, tested in 1897, turned about 26% of its fuel into work.

120
00:10:50,990 --> 00:10:57,200
>> And it runs the same four strokes of intake, compression, power and exhaust.

121
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:03,220
>> So let's put the whole idea together.

122
00:11:03,300 --> 00:11:10,790
>> Draw in, squeeze, burn and push out. That's four strokes, in two turns of the crank.

123
00:11:10,870 --> 00:11:15,060
>> The squeeze makes it efficient, and knock sets the limit on the squeeze.

124
00:11:15,140 --> 00:11:21,280
>> Patented in 1862 by a man who never built one, made to work by Otto in 1876

125
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:24,870
>> it's still the cycle inside most car engines.

126
00:11:24,950 --> 00:11:30,620
It's strange to think all of that is happening under the bonnet, every time a car idles.

127
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:38,100
>> At 3,000 rpm, that's two turns and one push, 25 times every second.
